{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ftoo85vTduEbhVGAnV7xZM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Real LLM heterogeneity exists but moderate; bias mechanism confirmed\n2\t  at smaller magnitudes than synthetic; EC-HTE works but requires larger expert budgets\n3\t  than hoped; diagnostic reframed from setting-level threshold to subgroup-level screening\n4\t  tool. Paper framing: lead with exact identity (mathematical, not empirical), soften\n5\t  empirical headline to ''1.3-2x with current SOTA LLMs'', add ''When to Use EC-HTE''\n6\t  subsection with per-K budget recommendations, drop ''computable diagnostic threshold''\n7\t  from contributions and reframe as ''subgroup-level misclassification audit protocol''.\n8\t  Strengthen forward-looking argument: heterogeneity will increase on harder CSS tasks.\n9\t\n10\t  '\n11\timagined_experiments:\n12\t- name: Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n13\t  plan_id: exp-004\n14\t  description: 'Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. Map corpus\n15\t    structure to causal framework: T = comment contains identity-directed language\n16\t    (binary), Y = annotator severity rating (binarized at median), Z = LLM-classified\n17\t    hate speech type (e.g., dehumanization vs incivility — effect modifier), S = target\n18\t    demographic subgroups (race, gender, sexuality from corpus metadata). Annotate\n19\t    Z with GPT-4.1 API. Compare global-corrected vs EC-HTE CATE estimates across demographic\n20\t    subgroups. Report bootstrap CIs on CATE shift (global − EC-HTE) in SE units. Frame\n21\t    as pattern consistency, NOT ground-truth benchmark.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    n_samples: ~39000\n26\t    n_subgroups_demographic: 4-6\n27\t    n_subgroups_hate_type_z: 3-4\n28\t    gpt41_overall_accuracy_vs_expert: 0.72-0.78\n29\t    gpt41_delta_pi_across_subgroups: 0.06-0.11\n30\t    gpt41_misclass_rate_range:\n31\t    - 0.15\n32\t    - 0.32\n33\t    gpt41_worst_subgroup_error: 0.28-0.32\n34\t    gpt41_best_subgroup_error: 0.15-0.19\n35\t    global_vs_ec_hte_cate_shift_max_se: 1.4\n36\t    global_vs_ec_hte_cate_shift_median_se: 0.8\n37\t    subgroups_with_nonoverlapping_95ci: 1-2 of 4-6\n38\t    subgroups_with_overlapping_95ci: 3-4 of 4-6\n39\t    ec_hte_cate_direction_consistent_with_domain: true\n40\t    bootstrap_iterations: 1000\n41\t    qualitative_difference_detected: true\n42\t    quantitative_significance: partial\n43\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, real LLM heterogeneity is moderate (Δπ ~ 0.06-0.11).\n44\t    The UC Berkeley corpus has genuine demographic variation in hate speech perception,\n45\t    but GPT-4.1 is a strong annotator — its error heterogeneity across subgroups is\n46\t    measurable but not extreme. Most subgroups show directional CATE differences between\n47\t    global and EC-HTE corrections, but bootstrap CIs overlap for the majority because\n48\t    (a) the Δπ is moderate so the correction difference is small, and (b) per-subgroup\n49\t    sample sizes for smaller demographic groups (e.g., LGBTQ+ targets) yield wide\n50\t    CIs. 1-2 subgroups with the largest Δπ × Δτ product (likely race-related targets\n51\t    where both misclassification and effect heterogeneity concentrate) show non-overlapping\n52\t    CIs at 95% level. This supports the paper''s pattern- consistency narrative: EC-HTE\n53\t    produces qualitatively different and domain-consistent CATE distributions, but\n54\t    the real-data case study alone does not constitute strong quantitative evidence\n55\t    — it illustrates the method''s applicability and directional correctness. The\n56\t    0.28-0.32 worst-subgroup error rate reflects GPT-4.1''s known weakness on implicit/coded\n57\t    hate speech targeting specific demographics, while explicit hate speech (best\n58\t    subgroup) is classified more reliably at 0.15-0.19 error.\n59\t\n60\t    '\n61\t  tables:\n62\t  - name: 'Table: UC Berkeley Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup'\n63\t    columns:\n64\t    - subgroup\n65\t    - n\n66\t    - misclass_rate_llm\n67\t    - cate_naive\n68\t    - cate_global\n69\t    - cate_ec_hte\n70\t    - shift_se\n71\t    - ci_95_overlap\n72\t    representative_rows:\n73\t    - - race_target\n74\t      - 8200\n75\t      - 0.28\n76\t      - 0.142\n77\t      - 0.098\n78\t      - 0.131\n79\t      - 1.6\n80\t      - 'no'\n81\t    - - gender_target\n82\t      - 11500\n83\t      - 0.21\n84\t      - 0.105\n85\t      - 0.083\n86\t      - 0.097\n87\t      - 1.1\n88\t      - 'yes'\n89\t    - - sexuality_target\n90\t      - 4800\n91\t      - 0.25\n92\t      - 0.118\n93\t      - 0.089\n94\t      - 0.112\n95\t      - 1.3\n96\t      - marginal\n97\t    - - religion_target\n98\t      - 3200\n99\t      - 0.19\n100\t      - 0.091\n101\t      - 0.078\n102\t      - 0.086\n103\t      - 0.7\n104\t      - 'yes'\n105\t  figures_description:\n106\t  - 'Forest plot: CATE point estimates with 95% bootstrap CIs for each demographic\n107\t    subgroup under naive, global-corrected, and EC-HTE. Visual emphasis on subgroups\n108\t    where global and EC-HTE diverge beyond CI overlap.'\n109\t  - 'Bar chart: Per-subgroup LLM misclassification rates showing heterogeneity pattern.\n110\t    Annotate with Δπ values.'\n111\t  analysis: 'Results support pattern consistency: EC-HTE shifts CATEs toward domain-expected\n112\t    directions (hate speech targeting racial minorities shows larger treatment effects\n113\t    than global correction suggests, consistent with known annotation difficulty gradient).\n114\t    The limited number of non-overlapping CIs (1-2 of 4-6) is expected under median\n115\t    tier — real LLM heterogeneity is moderate, not extreme. This motivates the forward-looking\n116\t    argument: on harder annotation tasks (cross-lingual hate speech, implicit framing),\n117\t    heterogeneity will increase and EC-HTE''s advantage will grow. Frame in paper\n118\t    as ''illustrative application demonstrating practical relevance'' with explicit\n119\t    caveat that no ground-truth CATEs exist for real data. Move to ~1 page in main\n120\t    text with full table in appendix.\n121\t\n122\t    '\n123\t- name: Multiple LLM annotator comparison\n124\t  plan_id: exp-008\n125\t  description: 'Annotate TweetEval sentiment corpus (same as exp-003) with three LLMs:\n126\t    GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup\n127\t    misclassification rates for each LLM. Apply EC-HTE with each LLM''s annotations.\n128\t    Compare bias amplification patterns and EC-HTE benefit across LLMs. Show that\n129\t    EC-HTE benefit scales with LLM misclassification heterogeneity.\n130\t\n131\t    '\n132\t  expected_results:\n133\t    gpt41_overall_accuracy: 0.82-0.86\n134\t    gpt41_delta_pi: 0.07-0.10\n135\t    gpt41_bias_amplification_median: 1.4-1.7\n136\t    qwen3_overall_accuracy: 0.74-0.80\n137\t    qwen3_delta_pi: 0.10-0.18\n138\t    qwen3_bias_amplification_median: 1.6-2.2\n139\t    claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy: 0.83-0.87\n140\t    claude_sonnet_delta_pi: 0.05-0.09\n141\t    claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median: 1.3-1.5\n142\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt41_n500: 35-50\n143\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3_n500: 45-60\n144\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude_n500: 30-45\n145\t    rank_order_heterogeneity: Qwen3 > GPT-4.1 ≥ Claude Sonnet\n146\t    ec_hte_benefit_correlates_with_delta_pi: true\n147\t    correlation_delta_pi_vs_bias_reduction: 0.85-0.95\n148\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, real LLM heterogeneity is moderate across\n149\t    all models. Qwen3 as an open-source model has the highest overall error and the\n150\t    most heterogeneous error pattern — it struggles disproportionately on minority\n151\t    subgroups (short texts, hashtag-heavy content) compared to majority subgroups,\n152\t    yielding Δπ ~ 0.10-0.18. GPT-4.1 and Claude Sonnet are both frontier API models\n153\t    with similar overall accuracy (0.82-0.87), but GPT-4.1 shows slightly more heterogeneity\n154\t    (Δπ ~ 0.07-0.10 vs 0.05-0.09) due to different calibration patterns across text\n155\t    styles. The key finding is that EC-HTE benefit scales monotonically with Δπ: Qwen3\n156\t    benefits most (45-60% bias reduction), GPT-4.1 moderately (35-50%), Claude Sonnet\n157\t    least (30-45%). All bias amplification factors are in the 1.3-2.2x range — measurable\n158\t    but below the 2-3x synthetic headline. This confirms the median tier narrative:\n159\t    the bias mechanism is real and present in current SOTA LLMs, but at moderate magnitudes.\n160\t    The forward-looking argument gains support: Qwen3''s higher heterogeneity on this\n161\t    relatively easy task (sentiment) suggests that harder tasks will push all LLMs\n162\t    toward the higher end of the heterogeneity spectrum.\n163\t\n164\t    '\n165\t  tables:\n166\t  - name: 'Table: LLM Annotation Quality and EC-HTE Benefit Comparison'\n167\t    columns:\n168\t    - llm\n169\t    - overall_acc\n170\t    - delta_pi\n171\t    - bias_amp_median\n172\t    - ec_hte_bias_red_pct_n500\n173\t    - ec_hte_rmse_ratio_n500\n174\t    representative_rows:\n175\t    - - GPT-4.1\n176\t      - 0.84\n177\t      - 0.08\n178\t      - 1.55\n179\t      - 42\n180\t      - 1.35\n181\t    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n182\t      - 0.77\n183\t      - 0.14\n184\t      - 1.9\n185\t      - 53\n186\t      - 1.22\n187\t    - - Claude_Sonnet_4.6\n188\t      - 0.85\n189\t      - 0.07\n190\t      - 1.4\n191\t      - 37\n192\t      - 1.42\n193\t  - name: 'Table: Per-Subgroup Misclassification Rates by LLM'\n194\t    columns:\n195\t    - subgroup\n196\t    - n_pct\n197\t    - gpt41_misclass\n198\t    - qwen3_misclass\n199\t    - claude_misclass\n200\t    representative_rows:\n201\t    - - S0_long_no_hashtag\n202\t      - 38%\n203\t      - 0.11\n204\t      - 0.16\n205\t      - 0.1\n206\t    - - S1_long_hashtag\n207\t      - 38%\n208\t      - 0.14\n209\t      - 0.2\n210\t      - 0.13\n211\t    - - S2_short_no_hashtag\n212\t      - 12%\n213\t      - 0.19\n214\t      - 0.28\n215\t      - 0.17\n216\t    - - S3_short_hashtag\n217\t      - 12%\n218\t      - 0.22\n219\t      - 0.34\n220\t      - 0.2\n221\t  figures_description:\n222\t  - 'Scatter plot: Δπ (x-axis) vs EC-HTE bias reduction % (y-axis) with one point\n223\t    per LLM. Shows monotonic relationship. Annotate with LLM names. Include regression\n224\t    line with R².'\n225\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: Per-subgroup misclassification rates for each LLM. Shows heterogeneity\n226\t    pattern is qualitatively similar across LLMs but magnitude differs.'\n227\t  analysis: 'This experiment is P1 — likely moves to appendix under median tier to\n228\t    free main-text space. Key takeaway for main text (1-2 sentences): ''EC-HTE benefit\n229\t    scales with LLM misclassification heterogeneity; across three SOTA LLMs, bias\n230\t    amplification ranges from 1.3-2.2x, with open-source models showing higher heterogeneity\n231\t    and correspondingly greater EC-HTE benefit.'' The Qwen3 results provide the forward-looking\n232\t    argument anchor: even at moderate overall accuracy, subgroup-level heterogeneity\n233\t    creates meaningful bias. Full table in appendix. RMSE ratio > 1.0 for all LLMs\n234\t    at n=500 confirms the median-tier finding that EC-HTE requires larger expert budgets\n235\t    than hoped — bias is reduced but RMSE tradeoff is real.\n236\t\n237\t    '\n238\t- name: Adaptive expert annotation allocation\n239\t  plan_id: exp-009\n240\t  description: 'Implement three allocation strategies for expert annotation budget:\n241\t    (a) uniform allocation (baseline), (b) uncertainty-weighted (allocate proportional\n242\t    to posterior variance of CM estimates), (c) CATE-weighted (allocate proportional\n243\t    to estimated |CATE| × misclassification uncertainty — targeting subgroups where\n244\t    correction matters most). Compare on synthetic DGP from exp-001 at n_expert ∈\n245\t    {250, 500, 1000} × K=4 (where budget pressure is highest). Primary metric: RMSE\n246\t    of subgroup CATEs.\n247\t\n248\t    '\n249\t  expected_results:\n250\t    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n250: 8-15%\n251\t    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n500: 5-10%\n252\t    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n1000: 2-5%\n253\t    uncertainty_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n250: 5-10%\n254\t    uncertainty_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n500: 3-7%\n255\t    cate_weighted_vs_uncertainty_advantage: modest, 3-6% at n250\n256\t    worst_subgroup_rmse_improvement_n250: 15-25%\n257\t    allocation_shift_from_uniform: ~40-60% of budget to 2 highest-error subgroups\n258\t      (S2,S3) vs 25% each under uniform\n259\t    crossover_improvement: K4 extreme crossover moves from n=1000 to n=750-800 with\n260\t      CATE-weighted allocation\n261\t    adaptive_overhead_runtime: <5% additional compute\n262\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, the advantage of adaptive allocation over\n263\t    uniform is modest because (a) with moderate LLM heterogeneity (Δπ ~ 0.05-0.12),\n264\t    the per-subgroup CM estimation noise differences are not dramatic enough for targeted\n265\t    allocation to yield large gains, and (b) at K=4, even with optimal allocation,\n266\t    the smallest subgroups (12%) still have limited sample sizes that dominate the\n267\t    variance. The CATE-weighted strategy helps most at n=250 (8-15% RMSE reduction)\n268\t    where budget pressure is highest — it effectively moves the K=4 crossover point\n269\t    from n=1000 to ~n=750-800, but does not achieve the hoped-for halving of required\n270\t    budget. The worst-subgroup improvement (15-25%) is more meaningful than the average\n271\t    because allocation concentrates labels on S3 (highest error, smallest subgroup)\n272\t    where marginal expert labels have the highest impact on CM estimation quality.\n273\t    At n=1000+, all strategies converge because per-subgroup CMs are already well-estimated\n274\t    under any allocation. The modest overall gain reflects the median-tier reality\n275\t    that the problem is fundamentally about total budget, not allocation efficiency.\n276\t\n277\t    '\n278\t  tables:\n279\t  - name: 'Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison — K=4 Extreme Regime'\n280\t    columns:\n281\t    - n_expert\n282\t    - strategy\n283\t    - rmse_mean\n284\t    - rmse_worst_sg\n285\t    - bias_mean\n286\t    - allocation_S0_S1_S2_S3\n287\t    representative_rows:\n288\t    - - 250\n289\t      - uniform\n290\t      - 0.385\n291\t      - 0.62\n292\t      - 0.08\n293\t      - 25/25/25/25%\n294\t    - - 250\n295\t      - uncertainty\n296\t      - 0.358\n297\t      - 0.54\n298\t      - 0.07\n299\t      - 18/22/28/32%\n300\t    - - 250\n301\t      - cate_weighted\n302\t      - 0.34\n303\t      - 0.48\n304\t      - 0.06\n305\t      - 15/20/27/38%\n306\t    - - 500\n307\t      - uniform\n308\t      - 0.245\n309\t      - 0.38\n310\t      - 0.05\n311\t      - 25/25/25/25%\n312\t    - - 500\n313\t      - cate_weighted\n314\t      - 0.225\n315\t      - 0.31\n316\t      - 0.04\n317\t      - 16/21/26/37%\n318\t    - - 1000\n319\t      - uniform\n320\t      - 0.165\n321\t      - 0.22\n322\t      - 0.03\n323\t      - 25/25/25/25%\n324\t    - - 1000\n325\t      - cate_weighted\n326\t      - 0.158\n327\t      - 0.2\n328\t      - 0.03\n329\t      - 18/22/27/33%\n330\t  figures_description:\n331\t  - 'Line plot: RMSE vs n_expert for three allocation strategies at K=4 extreme. Show\n332\t    convergence at large n and divergence at small n. Mark crossover points where\n333\t    EC-HTE beats global.'\n334\t  analysis: 'P2 experiment — moves to appendix. The modest gains (8-15% at best) under\n335\t    median tier mean this is a ''nice to have'' refinement rather than a core contribution.\n336\t    Main text should mention adaptive allocation as a practical extension in 1-2 sentences.\n337\t    The crossover improvement (n=1000 → n=750-800 for K=4) is useful but does not\n338\t    change the headline message that K=4 requires substantial expert budgets. Worth\n339\t    including in appendix to show the method is extensible and to provide practical\n340\t    guidance for practitioners who want to minimize labeling cost.\n341\t\n342\t    '\n343\t- name: Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\n344\t  plan_id: exp-010\n345\t  description: 'Test EC-HTE under three misspecification scenarios: (a) noisy subgroup\n346\t    assignment — add 10-30% label noise to true subgroup indicators Z, (b) coarsened\n347\t    subgroups — collapse K=4 true subgroups into K=2 observed subgroups (merging heterogeneous\n348\t    pairs), (c) irrelevant subgroup variable — condition on a variable uncorrelated\n349\t    with misclassification heterogeneity. Compare EC-HTE vs global correction under\n350\t    each scenario. DGP from exp-001 (K=4 extreme regime, n_expert=500).\n351\t\n352\t    '\n353\t  expected_results:\n354\t    noisy_10pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 5-10%\n355\t    noisy_20pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 12-22%\n356\t    noisy_30pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 25-40%\n357\t    noisy_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global: true\n358\t    noisy_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global: true\n359\t    noisy_30pct_ec_hte_vs_global: marginal or global wins on RMSE\n360\t    noisy_crossover_noise_level: ~25-30% noise\n361\t    coarsened_k4_to_k2_bias_residual_pct: 30-50%\n362\t    coarsened_k4_to_k2_rmse_vs_correct_ec_hte: 1.3-1.6x\n363\t    coarsened_k4_to_k2_still_beats_naive: true\n364\t    coarsened_k4_to_k2_vs_global: EC-HTE still better but margin halved\n365\t    irrelevant_ec_hte_rmse_vs_global: 1.05-1.15x\n366\t    irrelevant_ec_hte_bias_vs_global: approximately equal\n367\t    irrelevant_hb_shrinkage_toward_global: '>90% shrinkage weight on global mean'\n368\t    irrelevant_harm_vs_global: minimal — HB partial pooling collapses to near-global\n369\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, EC-HTE shows graceful degradation under misspecification\n370\t    due to HB partial pooling, but the degradation is noticeable, not negligible.\n371\t    (A) Noisy subgroup assignment: 10% noise is tolerable because HB smooths over\n372\t    small perturbations — per-subgroup CM estimates are slightly noisier but still\n373\t    capture the main heterogeneity pattern. At 20% noise, the subgroup-specific CMs\n374\t    are substantially contaminated, increasing RMSE by 12-22%. At 30%, subgroup structure\n375\t    is so noisy that per-subgroup CM estimation adds more noise than signal — EC-HTE\n376\t    becomes marginal vs global. The ~25-30% crossover is consistent with James-Stein\n377\t    theory: when the signal-to-noise ratio of subgroup differences drops below ~1,\n378\t    pooling dominates stratification. (B) Coarsened subgroups: merging K=4 into K=2\n379\t    by pairing heterogeneous subgroups retains only partial heterogeneity structure.\n380\t    EC-HTE still beats naive (it captures between-pair heterogeneity) but misses within-pair\n381\t    heterogeneity, leaving 30-50% of the original bias uncorrected. The RMSE penalty\n382\t    (1.3-1.6x) reflects estimating a less informative but lower-variance correction.\n383\t    (C) Irrelevant subgroup: this is the key robustness test — if the subgroup variable\n384\t    has no relationship to misclassification heterogeneity, EC-HTE should not hurt.\n385\t    HB partial pooling correctly shrinks subgroup-specific estimates toward the global\n386\t    mean (>90% shrinkage weight), making EC-HTE approximately equivalent to global\n387\t    correction with only minimal overhead (5-15% RMSE increase from residual subgroup\n388\t    noise). This demonstrates that the HB framework is ''safe to apply'' even when\n389\t    the practitioner is uncertain about subgroup relevance — a key practical selling\n390\t    point.\n391\t\n392\t    '\n393\t  tables:\n394\t  - name: 'Table: Robustness Analysis — RMSE Relative to Correctly-Specified EC-HTE'\n395\t    columns:\n396\t    - scenario\n397\t    - parameter\n398\t    - ec_hte_rmse_ratio\n399\t    - ec_hte_bias_ratio\n400\t    - beats_global\n401\t    - beats_naive\n402\t    representative_rows:\n403\t    - - noisy_10pct\n404\t      - 10% label noise\n405\t      - 1.08\n406\t      - 1.12\n407\t      - 'yes'\n408\t      - 'yes'\n409\t    - - noisy_20pct\n410\t      - 20% label noise\n411\t      - 1.18\n412\t      - 1.3\n413\t      - yes (marginal)\n414\t      - 'yes'\n415\t    - - noisy_30pct\n416\t      - 30% label noise\n417\t      - 1.35\n418\t      - 1.55\n419\t      - 'no'\n420\t      - 'yes'\n421\t    - - coarsened_k4_to_k2\n422\t      - K=4→K=2 merge\n423\t      - 1.45\n424\t      - 1.4\n425\t      - 'yes'\n426\t      - 'yes'\n427\t    - - irrelevant_subgroup\n428\t      - uncorrelated Z\n429\t      - 1.1\n430\t      - 1.02\n431\t      - no (≈equal)\n432\t      - 'yes'\n433\t  - name: 'Table: HB Shrinkage Weights Under Misspecification'\n434\t    columns:\n435\t    - scenario\n436\t    - mean_shrinkage_toward_global\n437\t    - min_shrinkage\n438\t    - max_shrinkage\n439\t    representative_rows:\n440\t    - - correct_specification\n441\t      - 0.35\n442\t      - 0.15\n443\t      - 0.55\n444\t    - - noisy_20pct\n445\t      - 0.55\n446\t      - 0.35\n447\t      - 0.7\n448\t    - - noisy_30pct\n449\t      - 0.68\n450\t      - 0.5\n451\t      - 0.82\n452\t    - - irrelevant_subgroup\n453\t      - 0.92\n454\t      - 0.85\n455\t      - 0.97\n456\t  figures_description:\n457\t  - 'Line plot: RMSE vs noise level (0-30%) for EC-HTE, global, and naive. Show EC-HTE\n458\t    degradation curve and crossover with global at ~25-30% noise.'\n459\t  - 'Bar chart: Shrinkage weight toward global mean for each misspecification scenario.\n460\t    Shows HB automatic adaptation.'\n461\t  analysis: 'P2 experiment — appendix. The key message for main text is a single paragraph\n462\t    in the robustness section: ''EC-HTE degrades gracefully under subgroup misspecification\n463\t    due to hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling. When subgroup assignments are noisy\n464\t    (up to ~20% label error), EC-HTE retains its advantage over global correction.\n465\t    When the subgroup variable is irrelevant, HB shrinkage collapses to near-global\n466\t    correction, incurring minimal RMSE overhead (5-15%). Practitioners can safely\n467\t    apply EC-HTE without certainty that the chosen subgroup variable captures the\n468\t    full misclassification heterogeneity structure.'' The coarsened-subgroups result\n469\t    also motivates the practical recommendation to use fine-grained subgroups when\n470\t    available, since coarsening sacrifices 30-50% of the bias reduction. Under median\n471\t    tier, the crossover at ~25-30% noise is a meaningful boundary condition — it says\n472\t    EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification but not to severe misspecification,\n473\t    which is an honest and credible claim.'\n474\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbb56758-8489-46a6-b7ce-c44335796f70","uuid":"300e4d66-cc6b-4aaf-9283-ff6e28cbd530","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:13:32.017Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032758/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Real LLM heterogeneity exists but moderate; bias mechanism confirmed\n  at smaller magnitudes than synthetic; EC-HTE works but requires larger expert budgets\n  than hoped; diagnostic reframed from setting-level threshold to subgroup-level screening\n  tool. Paper framing: lead with exact identity (mathematical, not empirical), soften\n  empirical headline to ''1.3-2x with current SOTA LLMs'', add ''When to Use EC-HTE''\n  subsection with per-K budget recommendations, drop ''computable diagnostic threshold''\n  from contributions and reframe as ''subgroup-level misclassification audit protocol''.\n  Strengthen forward-looking argument: heterogeneity will increase on harder CSS tasks.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. Map corpus\n    structure to causal framework: T = comment contains identity-directed language\n    (binary), Y = annotator severity rating (binarized at median), Z = LLM-classified\n    hate speech type (e.g., dehumanization vs incivility — effect modifier), S = target\n    demographic subgroups (race, gender, sexuality from corpus metadata). Annotate\n    Z with GPT-4.1 API. Compare global-corrected vs EC-HTE CATE estimates across demographic\n    subgroups. Report bootstrap CIs on CATE shift (global − EC-HTE) in SE units. Frame\n    as pattern consistency, NOT ground-truth benchmark.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_samples: ~39000\n    n_subgroups_demographic: 4-6\n    n_subgroups_hate_type_z: 3-4\n    gpt41_overall_accuracy_vs_expert: 0.72-0.78\n    gpt41_delta_pi_across_subgroups: 0.06-0.11\n    gpt41_misclass_rate_range:\n    - 0.15\n    - 0.32\n    gpt41_worst_subgroup_error: 0.28-0.32\n    gpt41_best_subgroup_error: 0.15-0.19\n    global_vs_ec_hte_cate_shift_max_se: 1.4\n    global_vs_ec_hte_cate_shift_median_se: 0.8\n    subgroups_with_nonoverlapping_95ci: 1-2 of 4-6\n    subgroups_with_overlapping_95ci: 3-4 of 4-6\n    ec_hte_cate_direction_consistent_with_domain: true\n    bootstrap_iterations: 1000\n    qualitative_difference_detected: true\n    quantitative_significance: partial\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, real LLM heterogeneity is moderate (Δπ ~ 0.06-0.11).\n    The UC Berkeley corpus has genuine demographic variation in hate speech perception,\n    but GPT-4.1 is a strong annotator — its error heterogeneity across subgroups is\n    measurable but not extreme. Most subgroups show directional CATE differences between\n    global and EC-HTE corrections, but bootstrap CIs overlap for the majority because\n    (a) the Δπ is moderate so the correction difference is small, and (b) per-subgroup\n    sample sizes for smaller demographic groups (e.g., LGBTQ+ targets) yield wide\n    CIs. 1-2 subgroups with the largest Δπ × Δτ product (likely race-related targets\n    where both misclassification and effect heterogeneity concentrate) show non-overlapping\n    CIs at 95% level. This supports the paper''s pattern- consistency narrative: EC-HTE\n    produces qualitatively different and domain-consistent CATE distributions, but\n    the real-data case study alone does not constitute strong quantitative evidence\n    — it illustrates the method''s applicability and directional correctness. The\n    0.28-0.32 worst-subgroup error rate reflects GPT-4.1''s known weakness on implicit/coded\n    hate speech targeting specific demographics, while explicit hate speech (best\n    subgroup) is classified more reliably at 0.15-0.19 error.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: UC Berkeley Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup'\n    columns:\n    - subgroup\n    - n\n    - misclass_rate_llm\n    - cate_naive\n    - cate_global\n    - cate_ec_hte\n    - shift_se\n    - ci_95_overlap\n    representative_rows:\n    - - race_target\n      - 8200\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.142\n      - 0.098\n      - 0.131\n      - 1.6\n      - 'no'\n    - - gender_target\n      - 11500\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.083\n      - 0.097\n      - 1.1\n      - 'yes'\n    - - sexuality_target\n      - 4800\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.118\n      - 0.089\n      - 0.112\n      - 1.3\n      - marginal\n    - - religion_target\n      - 3200\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.091\n      - 0.078\n      - 0.086\n      - 0.7\n      - 'yes'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: CATE point estimates with 95% bootstrap CIs for each demographic\n    subgroup under naive, global-corrected, and EC-HTE. Visual emphasis on subgroups\n    where global and EC-HTE diverge beyond CI overlap.'\n  - 'Bar chart: Per-subgroup LLM misclassification rates showing heterogeneity pattern.\n    Annotate with Δπ values.'\n  analysis: 'Results support pattern consistency: EC-HTE shifts CATEs toward domain-expected\n    directions (hate speech targeting racial minorities shows larger treatment effects\n    than global correction suggests, consistent with known annotation difficulty gradient).\n    The limited number of non-overlapping CIs (1-2 of 4-6) is expected under median\n    tier — real LLM heterogeneity is moderate, not extreme. This motivates the forward-looking\n    argument: on harder annotation tasks (cross-lingual hate speech, implicit framing),\n    heterogeneity will increase and EC-HTE''s advantage will grow. Frame in paper\n    as ''illustrative application demonstrating practical relevance'' with explicit\n    caveat that no ground-truth CATEs exist for real data. Move to ~1 page in main\n    text with full table in appendix.\n\n    '\n- name: Multiple LLM annotator comparison\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Annotate TweetEval sentiment corpus (same as exp-003) with three LLMs:\n    GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup\n    misclassification rates for each LLM. Apply EC-HTE with each LLM''s annotations.\n    Compare bias amplification patterns and EC-HTE benefit across LLMs. Show that\n    EC-HTE benefit scales with LLM misclassification heterogeneity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    gpt41_overall_accuracy: 0.82-0.86\n    gpt41_delta_pi: 0.07-0.10\n    gpt41_bias_amplification_median: 1.4-1.7\n    qwen3_overall_accuracy: 0.74-0.80\n    qwen3_delta_pi: 0.10-0.18\n    qwen3_bias_amplification_median: 1.6-2.2\n    claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy: 0.83-0.87\n    claude_sonnet_delta_pi: 0.05-0.09\n    claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median: 1.3-1.5\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt41_n500: 35-50\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3_n500: 45-60\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude_n500: 30-45\n    rank_order_heterogeneity: Qwen3 > GPT-4.1 ≥ Claude Sonnet\n    ec_hte_benefit_correlates_with_delta_pi: true\n    correlation_delta_pi_vs_bias_reduction: 0.85-0.95\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, real LLM heterogeneity is moderate across\n    all models. Qwen3 as an open-source model has the highest overall error and the\n    most heterogeneous error pattern — it struggles disproportionately on minority\n    subgroups (short texts, hashtag-heavy content) compared to majority subgroups,\n    yielding Δπ ~ 0.10-0.18. GPT-4.1 and Claude Sonnet are both frontier API models\n    with similar overall accuracy (0.82-0.87), but GPT-4.1 shows slightly more heterogeneity\n    (Δπ ~ 0.07-0.10 vs 0.05-0.09) due to different calibration patterns across text\n    styles. The key finding is that EC-HTE benefit scales monotonically with Δπ: Qwen3\n    benefits most (45-60% bias reduction), GPT-4.1 moderately (35-50%), Claude Sonnet\n    least (30-45%). All bias amplification factors are in the 1.3-2.2x range — measurable\n    but below the 2-3x synthetic headline. This confirms the median tier narrative:\n    the bias mechanism is real and present in current SOTA LLMs, but at moderate magnitudes.\n    The forward-looking argument gains support: Qwen3''s higher heterogeneity on this\n    relatively easy task (sentiment) suggests that harder tasks will push all LLMs\n    toward the higher end of the heterogeneity spectrum.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: LLM Annotation Quality and EC-HTE Benefit Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - llm\n    - overall_acc\n    - delta_pi\n    - bias_amp_median\n    - ec_hte_bias_red_pct_n500\n    - ec_hte_rmse_ratio_n500\n    representative_rows:\n    - - GPT-4.1\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.08\n      - 1.55\n      - 42\n      - 1.35\n    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.14\n      - 1.9\n      - 53\n      - 1.22\n    - - Claude_Sonnet_4.6\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.07\n      - 1.4\n      - 37\n      - 1.42\n  - name: 'Table: Per-Subgroup Misclassification Rates by LLM'\n    columns:\n    - subgroup\n    - n_pct\n    - gpt41_misclass\n    - qwen3_misclass\n    - claude_misclass\n    representative_rows:\n    - - S0_long_no_hashtag\n      - 38%\n      - 0.11\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.1\n    - - S1_long_hashtag\n      - 38%\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.13\n    - - S2_short_no_hashtag\n      - 12%\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.17\n    - - S3_short_hashtag\n      - 12%\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: Δπ (x-axis) vs EC-HTE bias reduction % (y-axis) with one point\n    per LLM. Shows monotonic relationship. Annotate with LLM names. Include regression\n    line with R².'\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: Per-subgroup misclassification rates for each LLM. Shows heterogeneity\n    pattern is qualitatively similar across LLMs but magnitude differs.'\n  analysis: 'This experiment is P1 — likely moves to appendix under median tier to\n    free main-text space. Key takeaway for main text (1-2 sentences): ''EC-HTE benefit\n    scales with LLM misclassification heterogeneity; across three SOTA LLMs, bias\n    amplification ranges from 1.3-2.2x, with open-source models showing higher heterogeneity\n    and correspondingly greater EC-HTE benefit.'' The Qwen3 results provide the forward-looking\n    argument anchor: even at moderate overall accuracy, subgroup-level heterogeneity\n    creates meaningful bias. Full table in appendix. RMSE ratio > 1.0 for all LLMs\n    at n=500 confirms the median-tier finding that EC-HTE requires larger expert budgets\n    than hoped — bias is reduced but RMSE tradeoff is real.\n\n    '\n- name: Adaptive expert annotation allocation\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Implement three allocation strategies for expert annotation budget:\n    (a) uniform allocation (baseline), (b) uncertainty-weighted (allocate proportional\n    to posterior variance of CM estimates), (c) CATE-weighted (allocate proportional\n    to estimated |CATE| × misclassification uncertainty — targeting subgroups where\n    correction matters most). Compare on synthetic DGP from exp-001 at n_expert ∈\n    {250, 500, 1000} × K=4 (where budget pressure is highest). Primary metric: RMSE\n    of subgroup CATEs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n250: 8-15%\n    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n500: 5-10%\n    cate_weighted_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n1000: 2-5%\n    uncertainty_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n250: 5-10%\n    uncertainty_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_n500: 3-7%\n    cate_weighted_vs_uncertainty_advantage: modest, 3-6% at n250\n    worst_subgroup_rmse_improvement_n250: 15-25%\n    allocation_shift_from_uniform: ~40-60% of budget to 2 highest-error subgroups\n      (S2,S3) vs 25% each under uniform\n    crossover_improvement: K4 extreme crossover moves from n=1000 to n=750-800 with\n      CATE-weighted allocation\n    adaptive_overhead_runtime: <5% additional compute\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, the advantage of adaptive allocation over\n    uniform is modest because (a) with moderate LLM heterogeneity (Δπ ~ 0.05-0.12),\n    the per-subgroup CM estimation noise differences are not dramatic enough for targeted\n    allocation to yield large gains, and (b) at K=4, even with optimal allocation,\n    the smallest subgroups (12%) still have limited sample sizes that dominate the\n    variance. The CATE-weighted strategy helps most at n=250 (8-15% RMSE reduction)\n    where budget pressure is highest — it effectively moves the K=4 crossover point\n    from n=1000 to ~n=750-800, but does not achieve the hoped-for halving of required\n    budget. The worst-subgroup improvement (15-25%) is more meaningful than the average\n    because allocation concentrates labels on S3 (highest error, smallest subgroup)\n    where marginal expert labels have the highest impact on CM estimation quality.\n    At n=1000+, all strategies converge because per-subgroup CMs are already well-estimated\n    under any allocation. The modest overall gain reflects the median-tier reality\n    that the problem is fundamentally about total budget, not allocation efficiency.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison — K=4 Extreme Regime'\n    columns:\n    - n_expert\n    - strategy\n    - rmse_mean\n    - rmse_worst_sg\n    - bias_mean\n    - allocation_S0_S1_S2_S3\n    representative_rows:\n    - - 250\n      - uniform\n      - 0.385\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.08\n      - 25/25/25/25%\n    - - 250\n      - uncertainty\n      - 0.358\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.07\n      - 18/22/28/32%\n    - - 250\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.06\n      - 15/20/27/38%\n    - - 500\n      - uniform\n      - 0.245\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.05\n      - 25/25/25/25%\n    - - 500\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.225\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.04\n      - 16/21/26/37%\n    - - 1000\n      - uniform\n      - 0.165\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.03\n      - 25/25/25/25%\n    - - 1000\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.158\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.03\n      - 18/22/27/33%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: RMSE vs n_expert for three allocation strategies at K=4 extreme. Show\n    convergence at large n and divergence at small n. Mark crossover points where\n    EC-HTE beats global.'\n  analysis: 'P2 experiment — moves to appendix. The modest gains (8-15% at best) under\n    median tier mean this is a ''nice to have'' refinement rather than a core contribution.\n    Main text should mention adaptive allocation as a practical extension in 1-2 sentences.\n    The crossover improvement (n=1000 → n=750-800 for K=4) is useful but does not\n    change the headline message that K=4 requires substantial expert budgets. Worth\n    including in appendix to show the method is extensible and to provide practical\n    guidance for practitioners who want to minimize labeling cost.\n\n    '\n- name: Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Test EC-HTE under three misspecification scenarios: (a) noisy subgroup\n    assignment — add 10-30% label noise to true subgroup indicators Z, (b) coarsened\n    subgroups — collapse K=4 true subgroups into K=2 observed subgroups (merging heterogeneous\n    pairs), (c) irrelevant subgroup variable — condition on a variable uncorrelated\n    with misclassification heterogeneity. Compare EC-HTE vs global correction under\n    each scenario. DGP from exp-001 (K=4 extreme regime, n_expert=500).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    noisy_10pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 5-10%\n    noisy_20pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 12-22%\n    noisy_30pct_rmse_increase_vs_correct: 25-40%\n    noisy_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global: true\n    noisy_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global: true\n    noisy_30pct_ec_hte_vs_global: marginal or global wins on RMSE\n    noisy_crossover_noise_level: ~25-30% noise\n    coarsened_k4_to_k2_bias_residual_pct: 30-50%\n    coarsened_k4_to_k2_rmse_vs_correct_ec_hte: 1.3-1.6x\n    coarsened_k4_to_k2_still_beats_naive: true\n    coarsened_k4_to_k2_vs_global: EC-HTE still better but margin halved\n    irrelevant_ec_hte_rmse_vs_global: 1.05-1.15x\n    irrelevant_ec_hte_bias_vs_global: approximately equal\n    irrelevant_hb_shrinkage_toward_global: '>90% shrinkage weight on global mean'\n    irrelevant_harm_vs_global: minimal — HB partial pooling collapses to near-global\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, EC-HTE shows graceful degradation under misspecification\n    due to HB partial pooling, but the degradation is noticeable, not negligible.\n    (A) Noisy subgroup assignment: 10% noise is tolerable because HB smooths over\n    small perturbations — per-subgroup CM estimates are slightly noisier but still\n    capture the main heterogeneity pattern. At 20% noise, the subgroup-specific CMs\n    are substantially contaminated, increasing RMSE by 12-22%. At 30%, subgroup structure\n    is so noisy that per-subgroup CM estimation adds more noise than signal — EC-HTE\n    becomes marginal vs global. The ~25-30% crossover is consistent with James-Stein\n    theory: when the signal-to-noise ratio of subgroup differences drops below ~1,\n    pooling dominates stratification. (B) Coarsened subgroups: merging K=4 into K=2\n    by pairing heterogeneous subgroups retains only partial heterogeneity structure.\n    EC-HTE still beats naive (it captures between-pair heterogeneity) but misses within-pair\n    heterogeneity, leaving 30-50% of the original bias uncorrected. The RMSE penalty\n    (1.3-1.6x) reflects estimating a less informative but lower-variance correction.\n    (C) Irrelevant subgroup: this is the key robustness test — if the subgroup variable\n    has no relationship to misclassification heterogeneity, EC-HTE should not hurt.\n    HB partial pooling correctly shrinks subgroup-specific estimates toward the global\n    mean (>90% shrinkage weight), making EC-HTE approximately equivalent to global\n    correction with only minimal overhead (5-15% RMSE increase from residual subgroup\n    noise). This demonstrates that the HB framework is ''safe to apply'' even when\n    the practitioner is uncertain about subgroup relevance — a key practical selling\n    point.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Robustness Analysis — RMSE Relative to Correctly-Specified EC-HTE'\n    columns:\n    - scenario\n    - parameter\n    - ec_hte_rmse_ratio\n    - ec_hte_bias_ratio\n    - beats_global\n    - beats_naive\n    representative_rows:\n    - - noisy_10pct\n      - 10% label noise\n      - 1.08\n      - 1.12\n      - 'yes'\n      - 'yes'\n    - - noisy_20pct\n      - 20% label noise\n      - 1.18\n      - 1.3\n      - yes (marginal)\n      - 'yes'\n    - - noisy_30pct\n      - 30% label noise\n      - 1.35\n      - 1.55\n      - 'no'\n      - 'yes'\n    - - coarsened_k4_to_k2\n      - K=4→K=2 merge\n      - 1.45\n      - 1.4\n      - 'yes'\n      - 'yes'\n    - - irrelevant_subgroup\n      - uncorrelated Z\n      - 1.1\n      - 1.02\n      - no (≈equal)\n      - 'yes'\n  - name: 'Table: HB Shrinkage Weights Under Misspecification'\n    columns:\n    - scenario\n    - mean_shrinkage_toward_global\n    - min_shrinkage\n    - max_shrinkage\n    representative_rows:\n    - - correct_specification\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.55\n    - - noisy_20pct\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.7\n    - - noisy_30pct\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.82\n    - - irrelevant_subgroup\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.97\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: RMSE vs noise level (0-30%) for EC-HTE, global, and naive. Show EC-HTE\n    degradation curve and crossover with global at ~25-30% noise.'\n  - 'Bar chart: Shrinkage weight toward global mean for each misspecification scenario.\n    Shows HB automatic adaptation.'\n  analysis: 'P2 experiment — appendix. The key message for main text is a single paragraph\n    in the robustness section: ''EC-HTE degrades gracefully under subgroup misspecification\n    due to hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling. When subgroup assignments are noisy\n    (up to ~20% label error), EC-HTE retains its advantage over global correction.\n    When the subgroup variable is irrelevant, HB shrinkage collapses to near-global\n    correction, incurring minimal RMSE overhead (5-15%). Practitioners can safely\n    apply EC-HTE without certainty that the chosen subgroup variable captures the\n    full misclassification heterogeneity structure.'' The coarsened-subgroups result\n    also motivates the practical recommendation to use fine-grained subgroups when\n    available, since coarsening sacrifices 30-50% of the bias reduction. Under median\n    tier, the crossover at ~25-30% noise is a meaningful boundary condition — it says\n    EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification but not to severe misspecification,\n    which is an honest and credible claim.'\n","numLines":474,"startLine":1,"totalLines":474}}}
